import equational_theories.Definability.CloneExtra3

/-!
# Clone certificates from carriers the enumerators cannot reach

`Definability/CloneExtra3.lean` came from enumerating every magma on `Fin 3`, `Fin 4` and `Fin 5`
that models a given source law. Enumeration stops being possible somewhere around `Fin 6`, and
carrying it further mostly turns up witnesses whose clones are far too rich to write down -- the
family in `Magma.IsCloneFamily` has to contain the whole clone, because no proper subfamily of a
clone is closed under composition, and the composition table is checked entry by entry. Four
thousand operations is a fact about the board that cannot be recorded as a proof.

The witnesses here were found the other way round, by growing the clone of every magma in the bank
of models collected for the *positive* search and keeping the ones that close small. That bank
reaches `Fin 9` and `Fin 11`.

`Magma.negCert38` is the reason it was worth doing: a Latin square on `Fin 9` whose clone has nine
members. Its rows are permutations, so it has no constant term operation, which is what lets it
refute targets whose two sides are both compound -- those are satisfied by a constant operation and
so are invisible to most certificates. `Magma.negCert36` is poorer still, eleven operations on
`Fin 5`, and misses almost every target on the board; what limits such a witness is not the targets
it misses but the sources it models, and it models a single law.

Like every clone certificate these refute `TermDefinableFrom` and say nothing about
`DefinableFrom`; the magmas are finite, so they bear on the finite-magma variant too.

This file is generated by `genclone5.py`.
-/

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000

open Law Law.MagmaLaw

namespace Magma

